<BaseLayout title="About — The Fine Print" description="About this publication and its author, Achintyarup Ray.">
  <div class="shell flow">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p class="standfirst">
      What was sanctioned, what was built, and what got left out of the record.
    </p>

    <p>
      Most of what matters is in a document somebody filed and nobody read.
      A scheme approved in 1981 with seven units, of which three were built.
      A parliamentary report mimeographed in 1979 and never tabled. A court
      judgment that turns out not to say the thing everyone repeats about it.
      A support price announced every year for a crop the state has no
      intention of buying.
    </p>

    <p>
      This publication goes to those documents. The subjects range — food
      policy, environment, history, religion, literature, whatever the record
      turns out to hold — but the method does not. Claims here rest on primary
      sources where possible: judgments, gazette notifications, archival files,
      RTI replies, audited accounts. Where something rests on secondary
      reporting, it says so, and where a figure needs confirming, it says that too.
    </p>

    <h2>Who writes it</h2>

    <p>
      I am Achintyarup Ray, a journalist in Kolkata with thirty years on
      newspaper desks — The Times of India, Hindustan Times, The Statesman,
      Ei Samay. I translate between Bengali and English, and I write books for
      children. Some of what appears here began as reporting for a newspaper
      and outgrew the space available.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>

    <p>
      If something here is wrong, tell me. I will fix it in the open, with a
      note on the piece saying what changed and when. Documents are especially
      welcome — if you are sitting on a file, an order, or a reply that ought
      to be read, <a href="/contact">write to me</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
